-- title:
--   Scheja–Storch: the Bezoutian dualises a finite free complete intersection
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, let $m$ be a natural number, and let $f : \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to R[x_1,\dots,x_m]$ be a family of $m$ polynomials in $m$ variables; write $A := R[x_1,\dots,x_m]/(f_1,\dots,f_m)$ for the quotient by the ideal generated by the range of $f$, and assume that $A$ is finite and free as an $R$-module. Let $a = (a_{ij})$ be an $m \times m$ matrix with entries in $R[x]\otimes_R R[x]$ satisfying the Bezout identity $$f_i \otimes 1 - 1 \otimes f_i = \sum_{j} a_{ij}\,(x_j \otimes 1 - 1 \otimes x_j)$$ in $R[x]\otimes_R R[x]$ for every $i$. Let $\bar\Delta \in A \otimes_R A$ be the image of $\det(a_{ij})$ under the ring map $R[x]\otimes_R R[x] \to A \otimes_R A$ induced by the quotient map in both factors. The assertion is that the map $$\mathrm{Hom}_R(A,R) \longrightarrow A, \qquad \varphi \longmapsto (\varphi \otimes \mathrm{id}_A)(\bar\Delta),$$ contracting the left tensor factor and identifying $R \otimes_R A$ with $A$ by the left unitor, is bijective. No hypothesis of regularity, flatness or non-degeneracy beyond finite freeness of $A$ and the Bezout identity for $a$ is imposed, and no claim is made that $\bar\Delta$ is independent of the chosen matrix $a$.
--
--   This is the Scheja–Storch description of the canonical dualising element of a finite free complete intersection presented by $m$ equations in $m$ variables: the Bezoutian $\bar\Delta$ generates $A$ as a module over the $R$-dual of $A$, so $A$ is a Frobenius (Gorenstein) $R$-algebra with explicit generator. It is used to produce the $R$-linear trace form and its comparison with the Jacobian determinant in [`Algebra.exists_dual_bijective_mul_and_trace_eq_jacobianDet_mul_of_square_presentation`](thm.html#Algebra.exists_dual_bijective_mul_and_trace_eq_jacobianDet_mul_of_square_presentation).
